<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Qasimid State</h1><p class="st-meta">1597 CE – 1872 CE · capital Sanaa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Zaydi imamate that drove the Ottomans out of Yemen in 1636 and briefly united the country while Mocha coffee reached Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1597 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasim-ibn-muhammad">al-Qasim ibn Muhammad</a>, a Zaydi scholar, proclaimed himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> and began a revolt against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yemen-eyalet">Yemen Eyalet</a> from the northern mountains. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-muayyad-muhammad">al-Mu'ayyad Muhammad</a> continued the war until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> evacuated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a> in 1636, leaving the Qasimid family masters of the country.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutawakkil-ismail">al-Mutawakkil Isma'il</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> from 1644 to 1676, the state reached its greatest extent, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-lahej">Lahej</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hadramawt">Hadramawt</a>. Its wealth came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, shipped through <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mohu">Mocha</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English and French factories, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Although Zaydi doctrine called for elected imams, succession became dynastic, and the court at Sanaa or al-Mawahib resembled a monarchy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the early 18th century southern and eastern districts broke away, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-lahej">Sultanate of Lahej</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> exports fell as colonial plantations grew. Rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>, tribal revolts and Wahhabi raids weakened it further. In 1872 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Sanaa and formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yemen-vilayet">Yemen Vilayet</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/qasimid-state.jpg" alt="Qasimid State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: MoeyElan · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qasimid_State.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1597 CE – 1872 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sanaa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, al-Mu'ayyad Muhammad, al-Mutawakkil Isma'il</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zaydi Shi'a Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a>, <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qasimid Imamate, Zaydi Imamate (Qasimid), Al-Qasimi dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1734&amp;focus=qasimid-state" aria-label="Qasimid State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5887 1607 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Qasimid State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6207 1863L6186 1938M6207 1863L5709 970M6207 1863L6247 1946M6207 1863L6330 1840"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6186 1938h0M5709 970h0M6247 1946h0M6330 1840h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6207 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6207 1863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sanaa. <a href="/atlas?year=1734&amp;focus=qasimid-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
